cartridge "PLINK AND PLONK/WALTER E. MEYERS/1987"

' PLINK AND PLONK
' A ColecoVision game based on "Plink & Plonk" (version 3.0) by Walter E.
' Meyers, Ahoy! July 1987 (Commodore 64, BASIC and a little machine code,
' disk ahoy1987-07). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (the original's rules, read from its listing, orig/plink.bas):
' a 5 x 5 board. Player 1 has 12 Plinks, player 2 has 12 Plonks. First
' the players take turns placing one piece at a time on any empty cell but
' the centre (the "forbidden zone"), until the 24 pieces fill the board
' round the empty centre (lines 1000-1110). Then they take turns, Plink
' first, moving one of their pieces one cell in any of the eight
' directions to an empty cell (1200-1370). A move captures: in each of the
' eight directions from the cell moved to, one enemy piece, or two in a
' row, with one of the mover's pieces just beyond them, are removed
' (2000-2380, 2500-2520). Only the mover captures: stepping between two
' enemies is safe. A player left with one piece loses (2400-2420).
'
' From the original: the board, the placement with the forbidden centre,
' the one-step move in eight directions, the capture of one or two pieces
' in a line, Plink first both times, the win at one piece left, the
' messages (SOMEONE'S THERE NOW, FORBIDDEN ZONE, YOU DON'T HAVE ONE THERE,
' YOU BEGAN THERE, CAN'T MOVE THAT FAR), the caught pieces shown in grey
' before they vanish, the two players' colours (yellow and green), the
' plink and plonk sounds of placing and moving.
'
' Liberties: the original is for two players only; here there is also a
' one-player game against the computer (it plays the Plonks: it places
' next to its own pieces, and moves looking two moves ahead, its
' captures against the best capture you could answer with, plus a little
' chance). The original has no rule for a player who cannot move (it
' would wait for ever): that player passes. It also has no end but a
' capture win, and two careful players can shuffle for ever, so a game
' also ends after 50 moves in a row without a capture: more pieces wins,
' equal is a draw (the count is on screen). A piece with nowhere to go is
' refused when picked, and picking another of your pieces while one is
' picked changes the pick (the original put it back and asked again).
' Tile pictures of the two faces on a checker board instead of PETSCII,
' a sprite cursor that glides, the moving piece glides as a sprite, a
' tally of pieces, sounds, a title tune; the title's bouncing sprites and
' tune are replaced by an original tune; the play-again question is
' replaced by the house ending.
'
' Controls: the stick moves the cursor one cell (hold to repeat), either
' button picks. One player: either controller plays the Plinks. Two
' players: controller 1 Plinks, controller 2 Plonks. On the title, up and
' down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a
' wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune played
' once. `set sprite count` always given. Own xorshift random numbers.
' Nothing flashes.

const TS0000 = 128
const TS0001 = 129
